{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The integral of the conditional density $P(y|x)$ with respect to $y$ equals 1",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:09:08\u201300:10:00]: 'So the integral of conditional density, P of y given x with respect to y. What will it result to? Well, with respect to y, this is just probability density function. And the integral of probability density function always results in one.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ can be expressed as $\\int P(x|y)P(y) \\, dy$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:09:08\u201300:10:00]: 'What will we obtain in the left-hand part expression? So the integral of conditional density, P of y given x with respect to y. What will it result to? ... And this means that we obtain one equals P of x given y times P of y.' (Note: The transcript uses $P(x|y)$ but the claim uses $P(x|y)$; this is a minor notation difference but the meaning is consistent.)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Marginalization is equivalent to taking the expectation of $P(x|y)$ over $y$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:11:17\u201300:11:49]: 'So it is the same as to say that this is expectation with respect to y over all conditional distributions.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ can be derived by integrating the joint distribution over $y$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:11:17\u201300:11:49]: 'we take all different conditional distributions on x given different y's, we average them out with respect to y and we obtain unconditional distribution on x.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "This process generalizes to multiple variables: for $n$ variables, the marginal distribution over a subset can be obtained by integrating out the remaining variables",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:11:49\u201300:12:44]: 'by induction it's straightforward to prove that this goes through for arbitrary number of random variables so you have say distribution over x and x1 over x1 xn and we would like to get distribution over x1 xk we can easily do it just by taking joint distribution and integrating out remaining variables'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The term 'marginalization' in this context refers to the statistical concept of marginal distribution, not social marginalization",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From the notes: 'This lecture discusses statistical marginalization (mathematical process), not social marginalization. The term 'marginalization' in the transcript refers to the statistical concept, not social exclusion or discrimination.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ is related to the joint distribution $P(x,y)$ by $P(x) = \\int P(x,y) \\, dy$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:10:02\u201300:10:46]: 'Sorry, one equals the integral u of y given x, u of x given y, u of y, over p of x dy.' (This is a bit messy, but the idea is that the marginal distribution is obtained by integrating the joint distribution over $y$.)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginalization process involves averaging conditional distributions over all possible values of $y$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:11:17\u201300:11:49]: 'we take all different conditional distributions on x given different y's, we average them out with respect to y and we obtain unconditional distribution on x.'"
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The transcript uses $P(y|x)$ but the notes use $P(x|y)$; this is a common confusion in conditional probability notation. The notes correctly clarify that this is statistical marginalization, not social marginalization."
}
